---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="About Red Reactions | Entertainment News, Reviews & Lore" description="Learn about Red Reactions, an independent entertainment publication covering movies, TV, gaming, anime, comics, news, reviews and in-depth explainers.">
  <section class="wrap max-w-4xl py-12 md:py-16">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1 class="display mt-2 text-5xl sm:text-6xl">About Red Reactions</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg font-bold text-paper">No filter. Full context.</p>
    <div class="prose mt-8 max-w-3xl">
      <p>Red Reactions is an independent entertainment publication covering movies, television and streaming, gaming, anime, comics and pop culture. We follow the stories, characters, franchises and creative work that give people something to argue about, celebrate and keep thinking about after the credits roll.</p>
      <p>Our coverage mixes breaking and developing entertainment news with reviews, reactions and commentary, trailer coverage and breakdowns, character and franchise lore, explainers and deeper features. The goal is straightforward: make it easier to understand what happened, why it matters and what is actually worth your attention.</p>
      <p>Entertainment moves quickly, but audiences often need more than a quick reaction. Red Reactions is built for readers who want the immediate development and the surrounding context: the earlier story, the relevant source material, the uncertainty still on the table and the reason a particular moment has landed with fans.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <a class="card p-5 transition hover:border-red" href="/news/"><p class="eyebrow">Follow the story</p><h2 class="display mt-2 text-2xl">News</h2><p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">Breaking developments, official announcements and the context around them.</p></a>
      <a class="card p-5 transition hover:border-red" href="/reviews/"><p class="eyebrow">Read the verdict</p><h2 class="display mt-2 text-2xl">Reviews</h2><p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">Critical takes that explain the reasoning behind the score and opinion.</p></a>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-5 text-sm text-muted">Meet the people behind the coverage in <a class="font-bold text-red underline" href="/authors/">Our Writers</a>.</p>

    <div class="prose mt-14 max-w-3xl">
      <section>
        <h2>What We Cover</h2>
        <p>Red Reactions covers the big-screen events and smaller discoveries moving through entertainment culture. That includes <a href="/movies/">movies</a>, <a href="/tv/">TV &amp; Streaming</a>, <a href="/gaming/">gaming</a>, <a href="/anime/">anime</a> and <a href="/comics/">comics</a>, alongside trailers, news, reviews, reactions and Lore / Explained coverage. Some stories begin with a new trailer or casting announcement; others need a guide, a timeline or a closer look at the details people keep returning to.</p>
        <p>Major franchises matter because they shape the conversation, but they are not the only stories worth covering. Red Reactions is interested in the work, the audience response and the wider context that makes entertainment worth discussing.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Our Approach</h2>
        <p>We aim to combine clear reporting, useful context, informed commentary, entertainment knowledge and personality. A headline should tell readers what happened. The article beneath it should explain why the development matters, what remains uncertain and how it connects to the larger story.</p>
        <p>That also means being precise about the difference between confirmed information, official announcements, reports, rumors, leaks, speculation and Red Reactions analysis. Not every interesting claim has the same level of evidence. We do not promise perfect accuracy, but we do try to make the status of information clear rather than presenting possibility as fact.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Sourcing and Accuracy</h2>
        <p>When appropriate, Red Reactions prioritizes official studio, publisher and developer announcements, official trailers, interviews and other primary sources. We also use reputable entertainment and industry publications when they provide relevant reporting or context. Developing stories can change as more information becomes available, so an article may need to reflect new facts after publication.</p>
        <p>If a factual error is identified, it can be corrected. For more detail about how we approach reporting, sources, corrections and editorial standards, read <a href="/editorial-policy/">our Editorial Policy</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Reviews, Reactions and Opinion</h2>
        <p>Reviews, reactions and commentary are editorial opinions. They are distinct from straight news reporting, even when they cover the same film, series, game or franchise. A review is an argument, not a final ruling, and readers are not expected to agree with every conclusion.</p>
        <p>Red Reactions can be direct, critical, humorous or enthusiastic. That voice is part of the publication, but it does not remove the responsibility to represent factual information accurately. The best conversations about entertainment leave room for disagreement while still being clear about what is known.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Independent Entertainment Coverage</h2>
        <p>Red Reactions is independently created and operated. Independence matters because it gives the publication room to follow stories, ask questions and form opinions without pretending every subject deserves the same response. Any sponsorships, affiliate relationships or other relevant commercial relationships will be disclosed where applicable.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Behind Red Reactions</h2>
        <p>Red Reactions is independently created and operated. The publication is built around a simple editorial idea: entertainment coverage should be readable, honest about uncertainty and willing to bring context to the conversation without losing its personality. We do not present a fictional editorial team or unverified credentials; the work should speak clearly for itself.</p>
        <p>That approach also means recognizing the difference between reporting, criticism and fan conversation. They can inform one another, but readers should be able to tell when they are reading confirmed information, an interpretation or an opinion about the work.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Contact Red Reactions</h2>
        <p>Readers can get in touch about corrections, story tips, feedback or business inquiries. If you have something to share, use <a href="/contact/">Contact Red Reactions</a>. Clear feedback helps the publication improve, and corrections are always worth raising.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
